<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makea Takau Ariki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Makea Takau Ariki was a high chief of the Te Au o <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> district of Rarotonga, holding the Makea title from 1871. Foreign governments and visiting ships treated her as queen of Rarotonga. She was the chief of the Cook Islands Federation formed in the 1890s, and remained the leading ariki after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> annexed the islands in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/makea-takau-ariki.jpg" alt="Makea Takau Ariki" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Makeatakauariki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Makea Takau was born around 1839, the daughter of Makea Te Vaerua Ariki, eldest daughter of Makea Pori Ariki, and was adopted by her mother's brother Makea Davida. The Makea title of Makea Nui, one of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> of Te Au O Tonga, passed among her mother's generation: Davida held it from 1839 to 1849, then Te Vaerua until 1857, Makea Daniela until 1866 and Makea Abera until his death in 1871, when she succeeded him. In the 1860s she married Ngamaru Rongotini Ariki, a high chief of Atiu, Mauke and Mitiaro. The marriage was childless, and Beatrice Grimshaw, a journalist from Ireland who visited in 1907, described it as happy despite Ngamaru's violent temper.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s armed takeover of Tahiti in 1843 had left the ariki of the Cook Islands anxious for British protection. In the 1870s the islands were prosperous and peaceful under Makea, a shrewd negotiator who won good prices for exports and cut the debts that had built up before her accession. By 1882 four of the five ariki of Rarotonga were women. Fearing a French invasion, she petitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1888 and signed the treaty at the Para O Tane Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avarua">Avarua</a> on 26 October 1888.</p><p>The first British Resident, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-moss">Frederick Moss</a>, tried to help the chiefs form a central government. In 1898 Major W. E. Gudgeon was sent to prepare a takeover by New Zealand, in line with the ambitions of its prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-seddon">Richard Seddon</a>. Makea preferred annexation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but the islands were annexed by New Zealand through a deed of cession signed by five ariki and seven lesser chiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a long illness Makea died at midnight on 1 May 1911, aged 72, and was buried in the family graveyard in the palace grounds. Her chosen successor, Rangi Makea, was installed on 24 October 1911 but was not made head of government. The palace later fell into ruin, and from 1990 students of Auckland University and local volunteers restored it over three years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Makea Takau Ariki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Makea Takau Ariki</b> of Rarotonga was chief of the federation, presiding over a federal council and parliament of ariki and elected members, while the islands kept their own councils.</blockquote><a href="/state/cook-islands-federation">Cook Islands Federation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Makea Takau Ariki</b>, who held her title from 1871, was treated by visiting ships and governments as queen.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rarotonga">Kingdom of Rarotonga</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avarua">Avarua</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avarua">Avarua</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-21.21,-159.77&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Avarua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Makea Takau Ariki"><image href="/maps/globes/g210s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4225 4284h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4225 4284h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-21.21,-159.77&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
